Round gen_r7 complete. Both deliverables are in: `impl/gen_twiddle.c` (r6 engine + new fused fold-combine codelet, minimal ~120-line diff) and a full "Round gen_r7" section appended to `strategies/gen_twiddle.md`.

The round's story, briefly: I built the queued split-custody two-axes-per-pass design (split intermediate volume, zero-copy direct feed, in-register transpose handoff) — bit-identical outputs, and it **lost 6–35% at every cell** (4/4 same-core pairs each). The measured lesson, now in the record: the gathers are the software prefetch and L1 staging, and the chain's in-place custody was already DRAM-optimal. I reverted it and shipped instead my queued fold-combine codelet for combine radices 7/11/13 — whole-k1-loop, staging-free, expressions verbatim — giving **−6 to −8% at 49/77/91/98/121** (the surprise-draw composites) with outputs bit-identical to r6 everywhere and scored cells at confirmed parity (10 pairs at the one scare cell, L=50). All gates pass with r6's exact values; library API unchanged (frozen), gen_bluestein's adoption verified compiling.

Ended on: fused fold-combine codelet (r=7/11/13, staging deleted, bit-identical) after a fully-measured negative on split-custody direct-feed; best node-leased-core reads 8.927 µs/xform batched (L=12 B=64) and 10.104 µs/xform at B=1 (L=12), rel L2 = 2.959e-16 (worst across suite 4.8e-16, tol 1e-12).
